\subsection{Analysis choices made after results were known}\label{sec:S8.2}

Two noise conditions were named in the analysis configuration before any simulation: sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition, and the room field projected out. Whole-cortex results are reported in both conditions and regional results with sensor plus brain noise. No endpoint, test or margin of the original study was declared before its analyses. The exploratory spike endpoint (the dense array against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm with the practical detector) was chosen from << F.g4_family_per_opm_array >> comparisons per OPM array (Section~\ref{sec:S7.1}). The regions were chosen from the epilepsy literature (Section~\ref{sec:S4.2}), and rankings among them are exploratory. Top contact, the primary placement of the fixed helmet, was chosen after the adult's whole-cortex results were known. The laterally centered placement and the helmet scaled about the laterally centered head were added after the first pediatric results were known, to remove the templates' lateral offset; the << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements bound the first choice (Sections~\ref{sec:S5.1} and~\ref{sec:S5.2}). Further analyses were added after the main results were known, without changing the model: the check of the noise model against the measured Neuromag covariance and the sensitivity analyses (Sections~\ref{sec:S3.4}--\ref{sec:S3.6}), the MRI quality check of the school-aged children (Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}) and the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Section~\ref{sec:S5.3}). Each reproduces the results it builds on to within rounding. The confirmatory spike run was designed before it was run (main text, Section 2.5) and is the only pre-specified test of the study.

The model also changed while results were known. Earlier versions took the OPM sensitive axes from the adult's stored outer-skin normals, which are nearly radial, and placed the adult's head surface outside its MRI scalp, by a median of << F.g2_conform_median_mm >> mm; they also differed in how the OPM cell's clearance from the head was checked. The final model puts every head's surface on its MRI scalp, so that the clearance rule moves only the sites the anatomy demands and the OPM standoff is the same (<< F.g3b_all_dense_standoff_2dp_range >> mm) above every head's own MRI scalp (the templates' scalp convention differs from the adult's; Section~\ref{sec:S4.3}). It takes the sensitive axes from that surface's own triangles (Section~\ref{sec:S3.1}). This change to equal OPM standoff was made after the pediatric results were known. It lowered $\Delta$ by << F.g3b_v3_v4_delta_drop_range >> dB in the scaled adults and templates (the school-aged children exist only in the final model). It raised the adult's projected dense ratio (<< F.g2_hse_v3_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> with the earlier construction, << F.g2_hse_v4sites_v3axes_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> with the final sites and the earlier axes, << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> in the final model). It made the laterally centered scaled-helmet $\Delta$ of the scaled adults and templates negative. It strengthened the adult's exploratory spike result at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm (earlier << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm >> locations favoring the OPM/Neuromag, p = << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm_p >>, S50 ratio << F.g4_earlier_v3_dense_vs_combined_10_20mm_ratio >>; final << F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_locs_opm >>/<< F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_locs_squid >> and << F.g4_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_10_20mm_ratio >>). Every array change redraws the noise, because an array's channel count changes the random stream. The dense array's advantage at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm held in every version, but which deeper cells reach p < << F.wr_p_exploratory_threshold >> did not. The site-matched array's deficit at << F.g4_band_45_70mm >> mm in two earlier versions (<< F.g4_earlier_v2_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm >> and << F.g4_earlier_v3_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm >> locations, the latter with p = << F.g4_earlier_v3_matched_vs_combined_45_70mm_p >>) does not reach p < << F.wr_p_exploratory_threshold >> in the final model (<< F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_locs_opm >>/<< F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_locs_squid >> locations, p = << F.g4_adult_matched_vs_combined_practical_45_70mm_p >>). Results that depend on these choices are post hoc, the earlier results were not retained, and persistence across versions is not replication. The main text states the post hoc caveat once (Section 2.6) and reports every exploratory result as such.

\subsection{Literature search and parameter provenance}\label{sec:S8.3}

The clinical and modeling studies of main-text Table 3 were found by searching PubMed and Europe PMC on << F.lit_search_date >> for OPM studies of epilepsy. The searches combined optically pumped magnetometers, OPM-MEG or on-scalp MEG with epilepsy, interictal or epileptiform activity, seizures, children or simulation, and were complemented by author searches. We also screened the reference lists of \citet{feys2025}, \citet{schwartz2025} and \citet{shen2026} and searched the web for preprints. For each study we extracted the design, patients, regions, OPM technology, comparator, definition of the signal-to-noise ratio and results. Each extracted value was checked against the full text (version of record or accepted manuscript) for << F.lit_studies_record_full_text_n >> of the << F.lit_studies_n >> studies (<< F.lit_studies_clinical_n >> clinical and << F.lit_studies_modelling_n >> modeling). For \citet{feys2022} it was checked against the full text of the preprint, and for \citet{ren2025} against the abstract alone, because the full text could not be retrieved and was not read. For \citet{ren2025}, main-text Table 3 therefore gives the direction of the result only. The model's counterparts in that table come from the same simulations as every other result of the study.

The Neuromag noise values and the << F.meth_dewar_mm >>-mm spacing between the coils and the helmet surface were taken from the manufacturer's datasheet \citep{elekta_triux}. The << F.meth_prov_standoff_mm >>-mm standoff of the OPM's sensing center was taken from the description of the sensors by \citet{jas2026} and from device papers \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}. The << F.meth_prov_cell_mm >>-mm cell is a modeling choice, an averaging volume rather than a device cell, whose effect on the field at its peak is at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. Four further settings are declared simulation choices without a device specification: the primary OPM noise of << F.meth_prov_opm_asd_primary >> fT/√Hz (the middle of the << F.meth_prov_opm_asd_sweep >> fT/√Hz sweep), the << F.meth_prov_pack_mm >>-mm minimum OPM spacing (standing for a sensor package of << F.meth_prov_package_range_mm >> mm), the << F.meth_opm_bw_corner_hz >>-Hz OPM response and the $1/f^{x}$ spectrum of the background. Two inputs were read from the figures of the preprint of \citet{jas2026}. The bar values of its Fig. 8 were read from the << F.meth_prov_raster_dpi >>-dpi raster, with a reading precision of about ±<< F.meth_prov_fig8_precision_pct >>. Its empty-room OPM floor was read from its Fig. 9 as << F.meth_prov_fig9_db_range >> dB re fT²/Hz, which corresponds to << F.meth_prov_fig9_asd_range >> fT/√Hz if the unstated reference is 1 fT²/Hz.
